  "slug": "learning-body-interaction-fa56",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply flow matching and Schrödinger bridges to infer single-cell dynamics from snapshots, perturbations, and spatial transcriptomics data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learning generative dynamics that transport cell states over time, with flow matching and Schrödinger bridges as the shared mathematical machinery. Elysium is a simulation-free unbalanced Schrödinger bridge solver that jointly learns state transitions and mass change for reconstructing single-cell trajectories. HORIZON applies flow matching to spatiotemporal cell dynamics from spatial transcriptomics and removes rigid frame motion before modeling. MitoWorld is a stochastic world model that generates single-cell future images under gene knockdown, combining latent dynamics, a division hazard, and diffusion rendering. The fourth preprint is a methodological comparison of four interpolation paths for flow-based models of molecular conformations, including a new operator-based path tested on a toy potential, and it addresses path design rather than single-cell data. What is new across the set is explicit modeling of division, mass change, and perturbation response within generative trajectory frameworks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
